      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m sorry these comments are late. I threw in some ideas below. I\u0027m not sure they are good or bad.\n\nIt\u0027s been suggested we do not want to capture all resources. Is that true?\n\nAlso, some of my suggestions mean that playback doesn\u0027t match recording by which I mean, in JavaScript (or whatever the client language is), as a non-sense example, I might do\n\n```\n 1. encoder \u003d device.createCommandEncoder();\n 2. pass \u003d encoder.beginRenderPass();\n 3. pass.setPipeline(pipelineA);\n 4. pass.setPipeline(pipelineB);\n 5. pass.setPipeline(pipelineC);\n 6. pass.setBindGroup(0, bg0);\n 7. pass.setBindGroup(0, bg1);\n 8. pass.setBindGroup(0, bg2);\n 9. pass.draw(3);\n10. pass.end();\n```\n\nDo we want to want to record steps 3,4,6,7 even though none of them contributed to the result?\n\nThis comes up in many places. In JS we have `createComputePipelineAsync`. But from the point of view of capture, we only really need to capture pipelines that are used. (pipelines that are set via `pass.setPipeline` and then actually used via `pass.drawXXX` or `pass.dispatchWorkgroupsXXX` etc... In which case `createComputePipelineAsync` would never need to be recorded.\n\nBUT!! Is that in conflict with what the user wants to see? Does the user want to see that\n\n1. they called `createComputePipelineAsync`\n2. they waited\n3. they, at some point, called `pass.setPipeline` and `pass.draw`\n\nOr is it fine that the recording is only\n\n1. they, at some point, called `pass.setPipeline` and `pass.draw`\n\nAnd we retroactively serialized the pipeline creation and the resources needed to create it?",
